To restrict SSH connections on your Synology NAS by completely disabling password authentication, you must 
edit the SSH daemon configuration file and enforce key-based logins. This requires having your SSH keys already set up and tested.

Follow these steps to lock down SSH access to your Synology:

Step 1: Enforce Key-Based AuthenticationBefore disabling passwords, you must generate an SSH key pair on your computer and 
place your public key in the ~/.ssh/authorized_keys directory on your Synology NAS for the specific user.

Step 2: Edit the SSH Configuration
a) Open your terminal (or PuTTY) and SSH into your NAS as your administrator account.
b) Elevate your privileges to root by running:
sudo -i
c) Enter your administrator password again when prompted.
d) Open the SSH configuration file in the built-in text editor by running:
vim /etc/ssh/sshd_config
e) Locate the following lines and modify them so they read exactly as follows:
PasswordAuthentication no
ChallengeResponseAuthentication no
f) Save and close the file. 

Step 3: Restart the SSH Service
Apply the changes by restarting the SSH service via the Synology GUI:
a) Log in to the DSM web portal.
b) Go to Control Panel > Terminal & SNMP.
c) Uncheck Enable SSH service and click Apply.
d) Check the box again to Enable SSH service and click Apply.
